From ub756a7daa2c2aa6d at groups.google.com  Sun Aug 30 18:35:46 2015
From: ub756a7daa2c2aa6d at groups.google.com (Hadley Wickham)
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2015 18:35:46 +0000
Subject: Re: Is it possible to combine colour and size scales in legend?
Message-ID: <4S1ykIhqc5c._QxtM0bJFQAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <4S1ykIhqc5c.mV3W97J2FQAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <4S1ykIhqc5c.mV3W97J2FQAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/4S1ykIhqc5c/m/_QxtM0bJFQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You just need to tell ggplot2 to use a standard legend instead of a
continuous colour bar:

ggplot(some_data, aes(x = x, y = y, colour = c, size = c)) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_size(range = c(2, 5)) +

  scale_colour_gradient(low = "brown", high = "green4", guide =
guide_legend()) +
  coord_flip()

Hadley

On Sat, Aug 29, 2015 at 12:22 PM, Tom <tomh...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I have a data set with which I am creating a dot plot, and would like to map
> one of the variables to both colour and size (this seems to help
> interpretation and comprehension better than mapping the variable only to
> colour or only to size). ggplot2 insists on creating separate legends for
> size and scale; I'd like to combine them, as can be done in ggvis with
> add_legend(scales = c("size","fill")). Is this possible?
>
> Minimal eproducible example:
>
> # libraries used
> library(dplyr)
> library(ggplot2)
> library(ggvis)
>
> # example data, ordered for plotting in ggplot2
>
> some_data <- data.frame(x = factor(letters[1:10]),
>                         y = runif(10),
>                         c = seq(from = 0.4, to = 1.0, length.out = 10))
> levels_order <- some_data[order(some_data$y, decreasing = TRUE),]$x
> some_data$x <- factor(some_data$x, levels = levels_order)
>
> # the ggplot2 version, note separate legends
>
> ggplot(some_data, aes(x = x, y = y, colour = c, size = c)) +
>   geom_point() +
>   scale_size(range = c(2, 5)) +
>   scale_colour_gradient(low = "brown", high = "green4") +
>   coord_flip()
>
>  # the ggvis version, note colour and size are combined in the legend
>
> some_data %>%
>   ggvis(~x, ~y, fill = ~c, size = ~c) %>%
>   layer_points() %>%
>   add_legend(scales = c("size","fill"), title = "Earth similarity index")
>
>
> Alternatively, I might settle for the ggvis version if I could control the
> color, as I can with scale_colour_gradient() in ggplot2.
>
> Thank you,
>
> Tom
